  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1152 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nRest of the week into weekend (Sunday)...\n\nAs the shortwave trough swings through Tuesday into midweek, \nshortwave ridging will become squeezed in between trough over the \nmid to upper Plains while broad upper low sits over the \nnortheastern Gulf coast region. With ridging at the surface and \naloft centered over the Appalachians to eastern seaboard, light \neasterly flow and dry air will remain locked in through at least \nmidweek. This is a classic dry fall weather pattern with large \ndaily temperature swings, seasonably cool at night and mild in the\nday, and very little opportunity for getting rainfall. This could\nbring a touch of deeper moisture in late week, but more subtle \nmoist convergence associated with upper low shearing out and \nphasing with northern stream trough. Based on that, looks to\nremain a nice and dry week. Northern stream trough is much less \namplified and much more north in latitude, so rain prospects look\nless likely, with any significant rainfall potential unlikely. \nGiven the extremely dry conditions so far this fall season, we \nwill be monitoring wind/RH forecasts closely for increased \nwildfire danger. \n\nFor sensible weather, temperatures transitions from seasonably \ncool early week to seasonable warmth late week, some 5F to 7F \nabove Wednesday-Friday (highs of 70F to 78F and lows 39F to 49F \nTuesday night to 47F to 55F Thursday night) to 8F to 14F above \nnext weekend (highs of 68F to 80F and lows Saturday night of 50F \nto 57F). A weak front may bring in a touch cooler lows Sunday \nnight, still seasonably warm (41F to 53F). /DC/EC/",
